nodecode-fashio is a rewrite of fashiolabs's fashiolabs/fashio-agent-plugin:
https://github.com/fashiolabs/fashio-agent-plugin
commit 1901c1a576e752cbd1496e79006882d2f6c61a36
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Fashiolabs (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the capability the original's package points at — Fashio's
AI fashion photography on the user's own key. The original is configuration
only (an Agent Plugins v1 plugin.json and mcp.json naming Fashio's hosted MCP
server, plus one skill), and that server wraps Fashio's public REST API, so the
rewrite speaks that API directly from the cell: GET /v1/account for the diamond
balance and plan; GET /v1/models, /v1/fashion-models, /v1/venues, /v1/poses
and /v1/ready-template-sets for the catalogues a generation picks from;
POST /v1/uploads for an image, read by signature as the endpoint reads it;
POST /v1/generations and GET /v1/generations/{id} for a job and its result
URLs, with the same toolId, images, pose, ratio, customPrompt, motionPrompt,
targetRatio, jewelryType, shotType, stylePreset, backgroundColor and duration
body the documentation specifies, and a preset replayed through templateSetId;
GET, POST and DELETE /v1/template-sets for the saved presets; and
POST /v1/catalog-jobs for a curated ready set with optional shotIds, its per
shot charge and its pack progress. The credential is the same bearer API key,
read from FASHIO_API_KEY unless the section names another variable. The
skill's instruction to state the price and wait for the user's yes became
confirm: true on every call that spends diamonds, and on the delete.

What was left out: the MCP transport itself (initialize, tools/list,
tools/call and its thirteen tool descriptions), which is the harness's way of
reaching the same API and has no meaning once the tools are native; the
original's markdown skill and its Agent Plugins and Claude Code manifests,
whose prose lives here as (help :fashio), the tools' own descriptions and the
section's guide; the upload_image tool's base64 body, replaced by a local path;
and the three ready-set tools' separate wrappers, folded into
fashio_catalog kind:"ready_sets" and fashio_catalog_job. Nothing else of the
original is missing: the tool set is served by the remote server at run time,
so pinning it was never possible there either.
